    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d182">

                <pb facs="0297" n="1051" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp02v02/pg1051"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 659.119/78</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Denmark</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Egan</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Copenhagen</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-10-15T19:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 15, 1917, 7 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received October 16, 5.15
                        a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1398. The danger of Denmark’s being forced to make compensation to Germany to
                    secure fuel oil for the electric light and water works is growing. Even an
                    expression of opinion from our Government on the subject might help to allay
                    irritation and astonishment that an embargo, which can only injure our interests
                    here, continues. Our allies, the British, declare that it is not their
                    fault.</p>
                <pb facs="0298" n="1052" xml:id="pg1052"/>
                <p>The Danes are anti-German but their need of light and mechanical power grows more
                    imperative as the dark days approach. The provincial towns are clamoring for
                    relief.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Egan</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
